From cc456b60b11f89c4f6108c9634149a60ac8c15d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "kevin.krausse" <kkrausse97@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 03:43:25 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/11] parakeet decoder keeps Q8_0/Q4_0 weights packed; WASM
 builds compile the SIMD quant kernels

The TDT decode loop is mat-vecs over the LSTM and joint matrices, which as
fp32 mirrors (0.6b: 26 MB walked per token) are memory bound. When the model
stores them as Q8_0 or Q4_0 they now stay packed as Q8_0 blocks (Q4_0 is
rewritten losslessly: same scale, nibble - 8 as int8) and the CPU backend's
integer dot kernels run on them. TRANSCRIBE_DECODER_F32=1 restores the mirrors.

ggml-cpu: the Emscripten toolchain reports CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=x86, so
arch/wasm/quants.c was never compiled and every quantized dot was scalar
(packed weights were 2.5x slower than fp32 in WASM before this).

Native 1 thread, decode ms fp32 -> packed: 7 s 54 -> 20, 13.7 s 104 -> 36,
56 s 361 -> 121. Chrome (1 WASM thread): 7 s 80 -> 48, 56 s 547 -> 293.
Identical text on all three clips for Q4_0 and Q8_0. WASM heap in use 37 -> 16 MB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
